Version Control Using Git and GitHub
   1.Create a new repository on GitHub.
   2.Open Git Bash and check the installed Git version.
   3.Configure the Git username and email address.
   4.Verify the Git configuration using git config --list.
   5.Create a project directory using mkdir and navigate into it using cd.
   6.Initialize the directory as a Git repository using git init.
   7.Check the repository status using git status.
   8.Create a README.md file containing project information.
   9.Add the file to the staging area using git add.
   10.Commit the changes using git commit.
   11.Rename the default branch to main using git branch -M main.
   12.Connect the local repository to the GitHub repository using git remote add origin.
   13.Push the local repository to GitHub using git push -u origin main.
   14.Verify the uploaded files and commit history on GitHub.
   15.Perform further changes, create commits and push updates to demonstrate version control and collaboration.
git --version
git config --global user.name "name"
git config --global user.email "YOUR_EMAIL"
git config --list
mkdir GitProject
cd GitProject
git init
git status
echo "# My Git Project" > README.md
git add README.md
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com//practice.git
git push -u origin main
git remote -v
git status
git log --oneline
# For future updates
echo "Updated project information" >> README.md
git status
git add README.md
git commit -m "Updated README"
git push
